.TH PCM 2
.SH NAME
pcmdescdef, mkpcmdesc, pcmdescfmt, allocpcmconv, freepcmconv, pcmconv, pcmratio \- convert PCM audio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ta \w'Pcmconv* 'u
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <pcm.h>
.PP
.EX
.ta 6n +\w'Rune 'u
typedef struct Pcmdesc Pcmdesc;
struct Pcmdesc
{
	int	rate;
	int	channels;
	int	framesz;
	int	abits;
	int	bits;
	Rune	fmt;
};
.EE
.ta \w'Pcmconv* 'u
.PP
.B
Pcmdesc	pcmdescdef;
.PP
.B
int	mkpcmdesc(char *f, Pcmdesc *d)
.PP
.B
int	pcmdescfmt(Fmt *f)
.PP
.B
Pcmconv*	allocpcmconv(Pcmdesc *in, Pcmdesc *out)
.PP
.B
void	freepcmconv(Pcmconv *c)
.PP
.B
int	pcmconv(Pcmconv *c, void *in, void *out, int insz)
.PP
.B
int	pcmratio(Pcmconv *c, int insz)
.PP
.B
void*	emalloc(ulong n)
.PP
.B
void*	erealloc(void *p, ulong n)
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines convert a stream of PCM samples from one format, rate and number of channels
to another, as
.IR audio (1)'s
.I pcmconv
and
.I mixfs
do.
.PP
A
.B Pcmdesc
describes a stream.
It has
.I rate
frames a second, a frame holds a sample for each of its
.I channels
in
.I framesz
bytes.
.I Bits
is the size of a sample in bits, and
.I fmt
its format.
.I Abits
is the number of bits a sample has after the conversion reads it:
.I bits
for an integer, 16 for a-law and µ-law, and 32 for a float.
.PP
.I Mkpcmdesc
fills
.I d
from the string
.IR f ,
in the form that
.IR audio (1)
gives for
.IR pcmconv 's
.B -i
and
.BR -o ,
such as
.BR s16c2r44100 .
The letters
.BR s ,
.BR u ,
.BR S ,
.BR U ,
.BR f ,
.B a
and
.B µ
(or
.BR m )
name the format, and the number after it the bits;
.B c
gives the channels and
.B r
the rate.
A float has 32 or 64 bits, a-law and µ-law 8, and an integer 1 to 32.
.I Mkpcmdesc
returns 0, or \-1 if the string is not a format, and sets the error string.
.I Pcmdescdef
is
.BR s16c2r44100 ,
16-bit signed little-endian stereo at 44100 Hz.
.PP
.I Pcmdescfmt
is a
.IR print (2)
verb that prints a
.BR Pcmdesc ,
passed by value, in the form
.I mkpcmdesc
reads.
Install it with
.IR fmtinstall .
.PP
.I Allocpcmconv
makes a conversion from the stream
.I in
describes to the one
.I out
describes.
It returns nil and sets the error string if either has no channels, or if
.I out
is a-law or µ-law, which it cannot write.
.I Freepcmconv
frees a conversion.
.PP
.I Pcmconv
converts
.I insz
bytes from
.I in
and writes the result to
.IR out .
It returns the number of bytes it wrote.
It keeps a partial frame at the end of
.I in
and converts it with the next call's bytes.
If the two streams are the same, it copies the bytes.
Otherwise it reads each sample to an integer, adds dither if the output has fewer bits
and the input fewer than 32,
resamples with a filter if the rates differ, and writes the output's format.
If the input has more channels than the output, it mixes them all and writes the mix to each
output channel.
If it has fewer, it writes its first channel to each output channel.
.PP
.I Pcmratio
gives the most bytes
.I pcmconv
can write when it is given at most
.I insz
bytes, with the partial frame it may hold from its last call.
It returns \-1 and sets the error string if
.I insz
is less than a frame of the input.
.SS Memory
The library expects the program to provide two allocation routines,
.I emalloc
and
.IR erealloc .
They are
.IR malloc (2)'s
and
.IR realloc ,
except that they do not return if memory runs out, and
.I emalloc
sets the memory it returns to zero.
The library gets all its memory from them, and
.I freepcmconv
gives it back with
.IR free .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libpcm
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR audio (1),
.IR audio (3)
